+# Base USDC Yield Vault (v1)
+
+An ERC-4626 vault on Base. Users deposit **USDC**; the strategy pairs it with **WETH** in
+Aerodrome's `vAMM-WETH/USDC` pool, stakes the LP in that pool's gauge, and a keeper calls
+`harvest()` to claim the **AERO** emissions and compound them back into the position.
+
+```
+user USDC ──▶ YieldVault (ERC4626, shares = bvUSDC)
+                  │ keeper: pushToStrategy()
+                  ▼
+            AerodromeUsdcWethStrategy
+                  │ swap half USDC → WETH ──▶ Aerodrome Router
+                  │ addLiquidity ───────────▶ vAMM-WETH/USDC pool
+                  │ stake LP ───────────────▶ Gauge  ──▶ emits AERO
+                  ▲                                        │
+                  └──── harvest(): AERO → USDC → LP ◀──────┘
+```
+
+| Contract | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `src/YieldVault.sol` | ERC-4626 accounting, entry/exit fees, deposit cap, pause, strategy management |
+| `src/AerodromeUsdcWethStrategy.sol` | LP entry/exit, gauge staking, harvest/compound, oracle guards |
+| `src/interfaces/IAerodrome.sol` | Minimal Aerodrome interfaces, each signature verified against the deployed contracts |
+| `script/Deploy.s.sol` | Deployment with onchain preflight checks |
+
+---
+
+## Why these integrations
+
+All figures below were read from Base mainnet on **2026-08-20, around block 50,243,125**, not
+taken from documentation or announcements. Every address is checked again at construction time by
+the strategy, again at deploy time by `script/Deploy.s.sol`, and again on every fork-test run by
+`test_LiveIntegrationAssumptions`.
+
+### Venue: Aerodrome
+
+Aerodrome is the deepest venue for this pair on Base and the only one that pays LPs a *claimable*
+emission stream, which is what makes a compounding keeper worth running at all. It carries more
+than half of Base's DEX volume and is the chain's largest DEX by both TVL and volume.
+
+A Uniswap v3/v4 WETH/USDC position on Base earns trading fees only. Fee income cannot be
+compounded on a schedule the way a gauge reward can — it accrues into the position continuously
+and, for v3, requires active range management. Aerodrome's gauge gives a discrete, claimable
+`getReward()` stream in a single ERC-20, which is exactly the shape a `harvest()` function wants.
+
+### Pool: `vAMM-WETH/USDC`, not the Slipstream CL pool
+
+| | `vAMM-WETH/USDC` (chosen) | `CL100-WETH/USDC` |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Pool | `0xcDAC0d6c6C59727a65F871236188350531885C43` | `0xb2cc224c1c9feE385f8ad6a55b4d94E92359DC59` |
+| LP representation | fungible ERC-20 | ERC-721 position NFT |
+| TVL | ~$8.35M | ~$8.07M |
+| AERO to gauge | 20,520 / week | 225,914 / week |
+| Range management | none | required |
+
+The v1 uses the **volatile AMM pool** because its LP token is a plain ERC-20: it can be held,
+staked, valued and split proportionally without any position-management logic. That makes a
+correct ERC-4626 vault tractable.
+
+**This costs real yield, and you should decide about it deliberately.** At the emission rates
+above, the vAMM gauge pays roughly **6.2% APR** on staked TVL, while the CL100 gauge pays an order
+of magnitude more against a similar pool TVL. The CL number is not directly comparable — Slipstream
+distributes emissions by *in-range* liquidity, so a passive wide-range position captures far less
+than the headline, and a narrow one needs active rebalancing and carries much sharper impermanent
+loss. But the gap is large enough that a Slipstream strategy is the obvious v2, and this v1 should
+be understood as the safe, simple version rather than the yield-maximising one.
+
+### Verified addresses (Base, chainid 8453)
+
+| Role | Address | How it was verified |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| USDC | `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` | `symbol() == "USDC"`, `decimals() == 6` |
+| WETH | `0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000006` | `symbol() == "WETH"`, `decimals() == 18` |
+| AERO | `0x940181a94A35A4569E4529A3CDfB74e38FD98631` | `symbol() == "AERO"` |
+| Router | `0xcF77a3Ba9A5CA399B7c97c74d54e5b1Beb874E43` | `defaultFactory()` and `voter()` match the two below |
+| PoolFactory | `0x420DD381b31aEf6683db6B902084cB0FFECe40Da` | `getPool(USDC, WETH, false)` returns the pool below |
+| Voter | `0x16613524e02ad97eDfeF371bC883F2F5d6C480A5` | `gauges(pool)` returns the gauge below; `isAlive(gauge) == true` |
+| vAMM-WETH/USDC | `0xcDAC0d6c6C59727a65F871236188350531885C43` | `token0()==WETH`, `token1()==USDC`, `stable()==false` |
+| Gauge | `0x519BBD1Dd8C6A94C46080E24f316c14Ee758C025` | `stakingToken()==pool`, `rewardToken()==AERO`, `rewardRate() > 0` |
+| vAMM-USDC/AERO | `0x6cDcb1C4A4D1C3C6d054b27AC5B77e89eAFb971d` | derived from the factory; used to route *and* to price the reward swap |
+
+The strategy never accepts a pool address as input. It reads `gauge.stakingToken()`, then asserts
+that pool is volatile, holds exactly USDC and WETH, and is the same pool the router's factory
+returns for that pair. Token ordering is read from the pool rather than assumed.
+
+The reward swap routes **AERO → USDC directly**. At the time of writing that pool quoted 482.20
+USDC per 1,000 AERO versus 476.04 via AERO → WETH → USDC, and it is far deeper (~$28M vs ~$2.8M),
+so the direct route is both cheaper and less manipulable.
+
+### What the position actually earns — and what it does not
+
+This is the one integration detail most likely to be assumed wrong, so it is worth stating plainly:
+
+> **A gauge-staked Aerodrome LP earns AERO emissions only. It does not earn the trading fees its
+> own liquidity generates.**
+
+Confirmed two ways. In `Gauge.sol`, `_claimFees()` pulls the pool's accrued fees and forwards them
+to `feesVotingReward` for veAERO voters. And onchain, the pool reports fees accruing *to the gauge
+address* (`claimable0(gauge)` and `claimable1(gauge)` were both non-zero), with the gauge pointing
+at `feesVotingReward = 0x14df87824a11DC27afF185D3149E05aaa4735f60`.
+
+So "compound whatever the position earns" means compounding AERO. `harvest()` claims it; there are
+no claimable trading fees to collect, and any code that tried to would be claiming someone else's.
+The ~6.2% figure above is emissions only, and is the whole of the position's yield.
+
+### One recent protocol change, checked
+
+Aerodrome announced **Predictive Allocation** on 2026-07-26, replacing weekly veAERO gauge voting
+with a demand-forecasting mechanism. This changes how emissions are *allocated between* pools; it
+does not change the LP-facing gauge interface. Verified at the current block: the gauge is still
+the canonical one for the pool, `isAlive()` is true, `rewardRate()` is non-zero and `periodFinish`
+is in the future. The practical consequence is that **this pool's emission rate can now move faster
+than it used to**, which makes the keeper's `minHarvestAero` threshold and the deposit cap more
+important, not less.
+
+---
+
+## Safety model
+
+The hard problem in this vault is not staking — it is that `totalAssets()` has to price an LP
+position in USDC, and any error there is directly exploitable by depositing or withdrawing around
+it.
+
+**Fair-LP pricing.** For a constant-product pool with invariant `k = x·y`, the reserves an
+arbitrageur would restore at true price `p` are `x' = √(k/p)` and `y' = √(k·p)`, so the pool is
+worth `2·√(k·p)`. That depends on `k` and on the price, but *not* on the current reserve split. A
+flash swap moves the split while leaving `k` untouched, so it cannot move the valuation — unlike
+naive `reserve · lp / totalSupply` pricing, which it moves a lot. `p` comes from the pool's own
+30-minute observation ring buffer (`Pool.quote`), not from spot. `test_FairPricingIgnoresReserveSkew`
+swaps 200 WETH through the pool and asserts the reported value moves less than 30bps.
+
+**Four bounds, each doing one job.** Collapsing these into a single "slippage" number forces it
+wide enough to admit legitimate price impact, which defeats the manipulation check.
+
+| Bound | Default | Guards against |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `maxDeviationBps` | 500 | Spot dislocated from TWAP — manipulation, or a stale oracle. Fails closed. |
+| `maxSwapReserveBps` | 25 | Our *own* footprint: caps one swap at 0.25% of the reserve |
+| `maxSwapImpactBps` | 300 | Router quote vs TWAP-implied fair output — fee plus price impact |
+| `slippageBps` | 100 | Execution vs the router's own in-block quote |
+
+`maxDeviationBps` is enforced at the **vault** boundary via `strategy.checkPriceHealthy()`, on every
+share mint and burn — including withdrawals that idle USDC alone could have served, because the
+share *price* still depends on the oracle even when the trade does not touch the pool.
+
+**Keeper operations and user exits are bounded differently, on purpose.** `maxSwapImpactBps` is
+enforced on `earn()` and `harvest()`, which can be split and retried, but *not* on user
+withdrawals. A large exit genuinely does cost its own price impact, and blocking the exit is worse
+for the user than charging them for it. Withdrawals keep the manipulation and slippage bounds, so
+they are still protected from a rigged pool and from being sandwiched.
+
+**Entry and exit fees exist to stop cost socialisation, not to earn revenue.** Moving USDC into a
+50/50 pool means swapping half of it, which costs the pool fee plus impact. That cost lands on
+whoever holds shares at the moment the keeper deploys — so without a fee, an arriving depositor
+charges their entry to everyone already in. The 30bps entry and exit fees are *retained by the
+vault* for the remaining holders. `test_LateDepositorDoesNotStealYield` asserts a newcomer cannot
+capture yield earned before they arrived and cannot dilute the incumbent by more than 25bps.
+
+**Other properties.** Unclaimed AERO is deliberately excluded from `totalAssets()`, so nobody can
+mint shares against rewards accrued before they arrived. The vault uses a 6-decimal share offset
+against first-depositor inflation attacks. Governance is `Ownable2Step`. `sweep()` cannot touch
+USDC, WETH or the LP token. `emergencyExit()` unwinds to USDC and blocks redeployment while leaving
+user withdrawals open, and it degrades rather than reverting when the pool is dislocated (see
+below).
+
+**The emergency path works during an emergency.** Unstaking from the gauge and removing liquidity
+are proportional operations — they return the position's share of the reserves whatever the
+current split is — so `emergencyExit()` always performs them. Only the WETH → USDC leg is
+price-sensitive, so that one is *skipped* while the pool is dislocated rather than dumping into a
+manipulated book. Whatever is left in WETH still counts in `totalAssets()` (priced at the TWAP)
+and is finished later with `convertStrandedWeth()`. A circuit breaker that jams the exit hatch
+would be worse than no circuit breaker.
+
+---
+
+## Capacity
+
+The binding constraint is pool depth, not the contracts.
+
+`earn()` deploys at most `maxDeployable()` per call — twice `maxSwapReserveBps` of the USDC
+reserve, about **$21k** at current depth. This is not a limit on deposits: anything above it stays
+idle in the strategy, still counts toward `totalAssets()`, and goes in on the next call. Deploying
+in tranches is strictly *cheaper* than one large swap, because impact grows with the square of
+trade size — splitting across blocks, so arbitrage re-anchors the pool in between, cuts the total
+cost roughly in proportion to the number of tranches.
+
+`Deploy.s.sol` therefore sets a **$250k default deposit cap**. At that size the vault is ~3% of
+pool TVL and a full unwind stays inside the impact bound. Raise it deliberately, and re-check
+against live pool depth when you do.
+
+---
+
+## Deployment
+
+```bash
+forge install
+forge build
+export BASE_RPC_URL=https://mainnet.base.org
+
+export VAULT_OWNER=0x...      # governance; a multisig in production
+export VAULT_KEEPER=0x...     # bot EOA that calls earn() and harvest()
+export VAULT_TREASURY=0x...   # receives the 10% performance fee on harvested AERO
+export VAULT_DEPOSIT_CAP=250000000000   # optional, USDC units (6dp); default 250k
+
+forge script script/Deploy.s.sol:Deploy --rpc-url $BASE_RPC_URL --broadcast --verify
+```
+
+The script refuses to run unless `block.chainid == 8453` and its preflight passes: the gauge must
+stake the expected pool, be the Voter's canonical gauge for it, be alive, still pay AERO, and the
+pool must have enough TWAP history. Deploying against a dead or migrated gauge is the failure mode
+most likely to go unnoticed until user funds are already in.
+
+`setStrategy` is `onlyOwner`, so the broadcaster must be `VAULT_OWNER`. If governance is a multisig,
+delete that line from the script and have the multisig call `vault.setStrategy(strategy)` afterwards.
+
+**Post-deploy checklist**
+
+1. `vault.strategy()` is the strategy, and `strategy.vault()` is the vault.
+2. `strategy.pool()` and `strategy.gauge()` match the table above.
+3. Seed a small deposit yourself and run one full `pushToStrategy → earn → harvest` cycle before
+   opening deposits.
+4. Transfer ownership of both contracts to the multisig and `acceptOwnership()` from it.
+
+---
+
+## Keeper operation
+
+The keeper holds no funds and cannot move funds out of the system. Worst case it is a liveness
+dependency, and the owner can rotate it with `setKeeper()`.
+
+### `pushToStrategy()` + `earn()` — deploy new capital
+
+```solidity
+vault.pushToStrategy();   // sweep idle vault USDC into the strategy
+strategy.earn();          // deploy one tranche; repeat while idleUsdc() > 0
+```
+
+Run when `vault.idleAssets() + strategy.idleUsdc()` is worth the gas. **Call `earn()` in separate
+transactions, ideally separate blocks** — batching them into one tx removes the arbitrage window
+between tranches and gives away the cost saving that chunking exists for. `earn()` reverts with
+`ExcessiveSwapImpact` if the pool has not recovered enough; that is the signal to wait, not to
+retry harder.
+
+### `harvest(minUsdcFromAero)` — claim and compound
+
+```solidity
+uint256 pending = strategy.pendingRewards();
+if (pending < strategy.minHarvestAero()) return;
+strategy.harvest(minUsdcOut);   // minUsdcOut from an independent price source
+```
+
+Claims AERO from the gauge, swaps it to USDC, takes the performance fee, and compounds the rest.
+
+`minUsdcFromAero` is an *additional* floor on top of the onchain bounds; whichever is stricter
+wins. Passing `0` is safe — the contract still enforces the TWAP and quote bounds — but a keeper
+with an independent price feed should pass a real number, because that is the only bound that does
+not derive from the same pool being traded.
+
+**Cadence.** Compounding more often is not automatically better: each harvest pays gas, the 0.3%
+pool fee, and price impact. `minHarvestAero` (default 1 AERO) is the floor; a daily or
+twice-weekly cadence is a reasonable starting point at current emission rates. Re-tune it if
+Predictive Allocation moves this pool's emissions sharply.
+
+### Monitoring
+
+| Signal | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `Voter.isAlive(gauge)` goes false | Emissions have stopped. Consider `emergencyExit()`. |
+| `harvest` reverts `PriceOutOfBand` | Pool dislocated from TWAP. Wait; it should self-heal. Persistent = investigate. |
+| `earn` reverts `ExcessiveSwapImpact` | Tranche too large for current depth. Wait, or lower `maxSwapReserveBps`. |
+| `harvest` reverts `NothingToHarvest` | Below the dust threshold. Normal. |
+| `strategy.idleUsdc()` stays high | `earn()` is not keeping up, or impact bounds keep tripping. |
+| `strategy.idleWethValue()` non-trivial | A WETH leg was left unconverted. Run `convertStrandedWeth()`. |
+| Deposits/withdrawals revert | `checkPriceHealthy()` is failing — the vault is correctly refusing to price shares. |
+
+### Emergency
+
+`strategy.emergencyExit()` (owner) unwinds everything and blocks redeployment; user withdrawals
+stay open throughout. If the pool was dislocated at the time, the position comes back partly in
+WETH — call `strategy.convertStrandedWeth()` (keeper) once the pool re-anchors to finish.
+`vault.retireStrategy()` pulls it all back into the vault. `vault.pause()` stops deposits but never
+withdrawals.
+
+Note that `setStrategy()` refuses to migrate away from a strategy whose `totalAssets()` is not
+zero, so any stranded WETH must be converted before a migration.
+
+---
+
+## Testing
+
+```bash
+export BASE_RPC_URL=https://mainnet.base.org
+forge test              # 63 tests
+forge test -vv          # with the logged pool/TWAP/yield numbers
+```
+
+Three suites:
+
+- **`test/fork/BaseFork.t.sol`** (23) — the real proof. Runs against Base pinned at block
+  50,243,000: full deposit → deploy → harvest → withdraw cycles, tranching, fair-pricing under a
+  200 WETH reserve skew, the dislocation circuit breaker, emergency exit while the pool is
+  dislocated, and a round-trip fuzz.
+- **`test/fork/StrategyAdmin.t.sol`** (18) — constructor self-validation against live contracts,
+  access control, parameter bounds.
+- **`test/unit/YieldVault.t.sol`** (22) — vault accounting, fee math, caps, pause, oracle gating,
+  inflation-attack resistance. No network needed.
+
+The fork suites `vm.skip` themselves when no Base RPC is reachable, so `forge test` still passes
+offline — but it is then only testing the vault, not the integration. CI should set `BASE_RPC_URL`.
+
+---
+
+## Known limitations
+
+1. **Yield is well below the CL100 pool on the same pair.** Deliberate; see above. The v2 path is a
+   Slipstream strategy with active range management.
+2. **Impermanent loss is unhedged.** Depositors take 50% WETH exposure. A USDC deposit does not come
+   back as the same USDC value if ETH moves; the vault does not hedge this and does not claim to.
+3. **Standing depositors see a small transient mark-down when new capital is deployed** — the
+   freshly bought WETH is marked at a TWAP that still lags the price just paid. The entry fee covers
+   the persistent component; the residual (bounded at 25bps in tests) unwinds as the TWAP re-anchors.
+4. **Very large single withdrawals are expensive**, bearing their own impact by design. Above roughly
+   1% of pool reserves, withdrawing in tranches is materially cheaper.
+5. **The keeper is a liveness dependency.** Nothing compounds while it is down, but no funds are at
+   risk and users can always withdraw.
+6. **After an emergency exit taken during a dislocation**, part of the position sits in WETH.
+   `totalAssets()` prices it correctly, so share accounting stays right, but withdrawals larger
+   than the idle USDC balance revert until `convertStrandedWeth()` has run.
+7. **Single-venue concentration.** All capital sits in one Aerodrome pool and gauge. An exploit in
+   the pool, gauge or router is a total-loss event. The deposit cap is the mitigation for v1.
+8. **Not audited.** This is a first version.
